        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>Google has been working for some time on a way to block old browser extensions in Google Chrome. This goes hand in hand with the <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/2438114/interview-ghostery-explains-why-chrome-is-working-against-ad-blockers.html\">switch from Manifest V2 to Manifest V3</a>, a newer and presumably more secure architecture for the popular browser.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>As early as March 2025, this rendered some extensions\u2014including popular ad blockers such as uBlock Origin\u2014suddenly unusable, even though it was still possible to access them with a workaround. By the end of June, however, even that will no longer work. A future release of Chrome will block all add-ons still based on Manifest V2.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>The decisive factor here is the \u201cExtensionManifestV2Disabled\u201d flag in Google Chrome\u2019s code, reports <a href=\"https://piunikaweb.com/2026/06/08/chro",
